@page "/guides/identity-sqlite"

<DocPageMeta Title="ShiftIdentity on SQLite and the seed"
             Section="Guides"
             Order="60"
             Status="DocPageStatus.Draft"
             Description="Host ShiftIdentity on SQLite for development and tests, fill it with SeedShiftIdentitySqliteAsync from C# or JSON, and replace the removed fake identity endpoints."
             Keywords="shiftidentity,sqlite,seed,seeding,SeedShiftIdentitySqliteAsync,identity seed,json,development,tests,fake identity,AddFakeIdentityEndPoints" />

<DocSection Title="What you get">
    <p>
        An app that hosts ShiftIdentity (internal hosting) runs on SQLite like any other Shift Framework app: register
        SQLite and create the schema as described in <a href="/guides/sqlite">SQLite for development and tests</a>.
        Sign-in, multi-factor authentication, password changes and the identity dashboard all work on SQLite, in
        memory or in a file. ShiftIdentity adapts its own tables to SQLite; you write nothing for that.
    </p>
    <p>
        To fill the database, call <code>SeedShiftIdentitySqliteAsync</code>. It writes countries, companies,
        branches, users and the rest from a C# object or a JSON file, so the app can be signed in to as soon as it
        starts.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="What is different on SQLite">
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>The schema must exist before the app starts.</strong> If an identity table is missing, the app
            stops at startup with a message that names the missing tables and says to call <code>EnsureCreated</code>
            before <code>app.Run()</code>.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Simultaneous requests for one user.</strong> On SQL Server, two sign-ins of the same user at the
            same moment wait for each other. On SQLite the second one may be refused with <code>409 Conflict</code>
            instead; the client can try again. Requests for different users are not affected.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Table names.</strong> ShiftIdentity's tables are in the <code>ShiftIdentity</code> schema on SQL
            Server. SQLite has no schemas, so they are named <code>ShiftIdentity_Users</code>,
            <code>ShiftIdentity_Brands</code> and so on.
        </li>
    </ul>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Seed from C#">
    <p>
        Call the seed after <code>EnsureCreated</code> and before <code>app.Run()</code>. It has no condition of its
        own: put it inside your own <code>if</code> for the environments that should be seeded.
    </p>

    <DocCodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@(@"using ShiftSoftware.ShiftIdentity.Dashboard.AspNetCore.Extentsions;
using ShiftSoftware.ShiftIdentity.Data.Seeding;

var app = builder.Build();

await using (var scope = app.Services.CreateAsyncScope())
    await scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<DB>().Database.EnsureCreatedAsync();

if (app.Environment.IsDevelopment())
    await app.SeedShiftIdentitySqliteAsync(new IdentitySeedData
    {
        Countries = [new() { Key = ""iq"", Name = ""Iraq"", ShortCode = ""IQ"", CallingCode = ""+964"" }],
        Regions = [new() { Key = ""krg"", Name = ""Kurdistan"", Country = ""iq"" }],
        Cities = [new() { Key = ""erbil"", Name = ""Erbil"", Region = ""krg"" }],
        Companies = [new() { Key = ""acme"", Name = ""Acme"" }],
        CompanyBranches = [new() { Key = ""erbil-hq"", Name = ""Erbil HQ"", Company = ""acme"", City = ""erbil"" }],
        AccessTrees = [new() { Key = ""admin"", Name = ""Admin"", FullAccess = true }],
        Users =
        [
            new() { Username = ""admin"", Password = ""OneTwo"", FullName = ""Admin"", CompanyBranch = ""erbil-hq"", AccessTrees = [""admin""] },
        ],
    });

app.Run();")" />

    <p>
        A record names another record of the same seed by its <code>Key</code>; users are named by their
        <code>Username</code>. A key is only a name inside the seed and is not stored.
    </p>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Seed from JSON">
    <p>
        The second overload takes JSON of exactly the same shape. Property names may use any case, enums are written
        by name, and an access tree is a JSON object.
    </p>

    <DocCodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@(@"if (app.Environment.IsEnvironment(""Test""))
    await app.SeedShiftIdentitySqliteAsync(await File.ReadAllTextAsync(""identity-seed.test.json""));")" />

    <DocCodeBlock Language="json" Code="@(@"{
  ""countries"":       [{ ""key"": ""iq"", ""name"": ""Iraq"", ""shortCode"": ""IQ"", ""callingCode"": ""+964"" }],
  ""regions"":         [{ ""key"": ""krg"", ""name"": ""Kurdistan"", ""country"": ""iq"" }],
  ""cities"":          [{ ""key"": ""erbil"", ""name"": ""Erbil"", ""region"": ""krg"" }],
  ""companies"":       [{ ""key"": ""acme"", ""name"": ""Acme"", ""companyType"": ""NotSpecified"" }],
  ""brands"":          [{ ""key"": ""toyota"", ""name"": ""Toyota"" }],
  ""companyBranches"": [{ ""key"": ""erbil-hq"", ""name"": ""Erbil HQ"", ""company"": ""acme"", ""city"": ""erbil"", ""brands"": [""toyota""] }],
  ""teams"":           [{ ""key"": ""support"", ""name"": ""Support"", ""company"": ""acme"", ""companyBranches"": [""erbil-hq""] }],
  ""accessTrees"":     [{ ""key"": ""admin"", ""name"": ""Admin"", ""fullAccess"": true },
                      { ""key"": ""viewer"", ""name"": ""Viewer"", ""tree"": { ""MyActionTree"": [1] } }],
  ""apps"":            [{ ""appId"": ""my-app"", ""displayName"": ""My App"", ""redirectUri"": ""http://localhost:5234/Auth/Token"" }],
  ""users"":           [{ ""username"": ""admin"", ""password"": ""OneTwo"", ""fullName"": ""Admin"", ""email"": ""admin@example.com"",
                        ""companyBranch"": ""erbil-hq"", ""accessTrees"": [""admin""], ""teams"": [""support""] }]
}")" />
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Seed rules">
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>What can be seeded:</strong> countries, regions, cities, companies (with a parent company), brands,
            departments, services, company branches (with their brands, departments and services), teams (with their
            branches and users), access trees, apps, users and company calendars. The security tables that sign-in
            fills as it is used are not seeded; each new user gets its security row from the seed, so it can sign in
            at once.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Sign-in needs a branch.</strong> Every user needs <code>CompanyBranch</code> and every branch needs
            <code>City</code>: a session carries the user's company, region and branch, and the region comes from the
            branch's city. A user's company, region and country are taken from the branch.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Passwords:</strong> <code>Password</code>, hashed when the seed runs, or <code>PasswordHash</code>
            and <code>Salt</code>. A new user needs one of them.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Access trees:</strong> <code>FullAccess = true</code> grants full access to every action tree the
            app registers. Otherwise give <code>Tree</code>: a JSON object in JSON, a JSON string in C#.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>IDs:</strong> any record may have an <code>ID</code>, which is kept. Rows without one get the next
            free ID.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Existing rows</strong> (a database in a file, seeded before): a record matches a row by
            <code>ID</code> when it has one, otherwise by username for users, app ID for apps, integration ID when
            given, and otherwise by name within its parent (a region within its country, a branch within its company).
            A matched row is left as it is. Set <code>UpdateExisting = true</code> on the seed to update matched rows
            to the seed's values instead. A list of links that a record leaves out, such as a branch's brands, is never
            touched.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>Several seeds.</strong> Calls run in the order you write them. A key is only known inside its own
            seed, so to reference rows from an earlier seed, list them again: they match the existing rows and are left
            alone.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>All or nothing.</strong> Each call runs in one transaction. A mistake in the seed (an unknown key,
            a duplicate, a missing required value, an unknown JSON property) throws <code>IdentitySeedException</code>
            listing every problem, and nothing is written.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>SQLite only.</strong> On any other database the seed writes nothing, logs a warning and returns a
            result with <code>Skipped = true</code>, so one <code>Program.cs</code> can use SQLite in development and
            SQL Server in production. SQL Server apps keep using <code>SeedDBAsync</code>.
        </li>
    </ul>
</DocSection>

<DocSection Title="Replacing the fake identity endpoints">
    <p>
        <code>AddFakeIdentityEndPoints</code> has been removed from ShiftIdentity. It answered the identity endpoints
        with one configured user and no database. Its replacement is the real identity on SQLite, seeded with the
        same user, app and access tree. Keep the identity registration your project already has (a project created
        with <code>dotnet new shift</code> registers it with <code>AddShiftIdentityDashboard</code> and maps it with
        <code>MapShiftIdentityDashboard</code>), switch its <code>DbContext</code> to SQLite, and seed:
    </p>

    <DocCodeBlock Language="csharp" Code="@(@"// Before
mvcBuilder.AddFakeIdentityEndPoints(tokenSettings,
    new TokenUserDataDTO { ID = ""1"", Username = ""demo"", FullName = ""Demo"" },
    new AppDTO { AppId = ""menu-demo"", DisplayName = ""Menu Demo"", RedirectUri = ""http://localhost:5234/Auth/Token"" },
    ""OneTwo"",
    [@""{ """"ShiftIdentityActions"""": [1,2,3,4], """"MenuActionTree"""": [1,2,3,4] }""]);

// After
builder.Services.AddDbContext<DB>(x => x.UseSqlite(""Data Source=MenuDemo;Mode=Memory;Cache=Shared""));
// ... the identity registration, unchanged ...

var app = builder.Build();
await using (var scope = app.Services.CreateAsyncScope())
    await scope.ServiceProvider.GetRequiredService<DB>().Database.EnsureCreatedAsync();

if (app.Environment.IsDevelopment())
    await app.SeedShiftIdentitySqliteAsync(new IdentitySeedData
    {
        Countries = [new() { Key = ""c"", Name = ""Demo Country"", CallingCode = ""+1"" }],
        Regions = [new() { Key = ""r"", Name = ""Demo Region"", Country = ""c"" }],
        Cities = [new() { Key = ""city"", Name = ""Demo City"", Region = ""r"" }],
        Companies = [new() { Key = ""co"", Name = ""Demo Company"" }],
        CompanyBranches = [new() { Key = ""b"", Name = ""Demo Branch"", Company = ""co"", City = ""city"" }],
        AccessTrees = [new() { Key = ""all"", Name = ""Everything"", FullAccess = true }],
        Apps = [new() { AppId = ""menu-demo"", DisplayName = ""Menu Demo"", RedirectUri = ""http://localhost:5234/Auth/Token"" }],
        Users = [new() { ID = 1, Username = ""demo"", Password = ""OneTwo"", FullName = ""Demo"", CompanyBranch = ""b"", AccessTrees = [""all""] }],
    });")" />

    <p>Two things behave differently, on purpose:</p>
    <ul>
        <li>
            <strong>The username must match.</strong> The fake signed in whatever username it was sent, as its one
            configured user. The real identity signs in only the users that exist, with their own password.
        </li>
        <li>
            <strong>An auth code needs a signed-in user.</strong> The fake issued an auth code for an app without a
            session. The real identity issues one only to a user who is signed in.
        </li>
    </ul>
</DocSection>
